  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a PLC?",
      "a": "A programmable logic controller is a rugged industrial computer that controls machines. It reads inputs from sensors and buttons, runs a program, and switches outputs like motors and valves, and it is built to survive dust, heat and vibration.",
      "asks": [
        "What does PLC stand for?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are PLC inputs and outputs?",
      "a": "Inputs are signals coming in, such as buttons, switches and sensors. Outputs are things the PLC switches, such as motors, valves, lights and alarms.",
      "asks": [
        "What does a PLC connect to?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the PLC scan cycle?",
      "a": "A PLC repeats the same loop over and over: read all the inputs, run the program from top to bottom, then update all the outputs. One scan usually takes just a few milliseconds.",
      "asks": [
        "How fast does a PLC work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is ladder logic?",
      "a": "Ladder logic is a way of writing PLC programs that looks like an electrical wiring diagram, with rungs like a ladder. Each rung is a rule: if these contacts are closed, switch on that output.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you program a PLC?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens when you press an emergency stop?",
      "a": "It cuts power to the dangerous parts of the machine through a dedicated safety circuit and tells the PLC to stop. The machine will not restart until the button is reset and someone deliberately starts it again.",
      "asks": [
        "What does an e-stop button do?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The first PLC, the Modicon 084, was developed around 1968 for General Motors to replace large panels of relays.",
    "A typical PLC scan cycle takes a few milliseconds."
  ],
